 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity**: Dominated by the hardware-accelerated ray-triangle intersection tests. The number of `optixTrace` calls is reduced by aggregating samples in the Any-hit shader [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity**: Low memory footprint due to geometry instancing (storing only one canonical icosahedron) [S1]. The instance buffer size scales linearly with the number of Gaussians [S1].
-*   **Performance**: On an NVIDIA RTX 5090 GPU, increasing the intersection quota from 16 to 128 IPR improves Avg. PSNR from 29.87 to 32.65 on the NeRF Synthetic dataset [S1]. Using Hash Grid initialization instead of random initialization yields a slight performance gain (32.65 vs 32.54 Avg PSNR) [S1].
-*   **Strengths**: Leverages dedicated RT cores for high-performance intersection testing; reduces software overhead by offloading logic to shaders; conservatively bounds Gaussians to ensure coverage [S1].
-*   **Limitations**: The proxy geometry (icosahedron) is a conservative approximation; the Mahalanobis threshold $\lambda$ must be tuned for scene type (bounded vs. unbounded) [S1].
-*   **When to use**: Suitable for implicit editable scenes requiring efficient sampling of 3D Gaussians, particularly in large-scale or bounded environments where hardware acceleration is beneficial [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Dominated by the number of OptiX trace calls and the sorting of the hit buffer. The hit buffer size is fixed at 16, so sorting is $O(1)$ per intersection. The total time depends on the number of intersections required to reach the IPR quota [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Low memory footprint due to geometry instancing (single canonical icosahedron) and fixed-size hit buffers [S1].
+*   **Performance:** On NVIDIA RTX 5090, increasing IPR from 16 to 128 improves Avg. PSNR from 29.87 to 32.65 on NeRF Synthetic dataset [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Hardware-accelerated ray-triangle intersection; reduced number of `optixTrace` calls via hit buffer accumulation; efficient memory usage via instancing [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Proxy geometry is an approximation; may miss samples if $\lambda$ is too low; requires OptiX support [S1].
+*   **When to use:** Implicit editable scenes with Gaussian primitives; when hardware ray tracing is available; when high-fidelity sampling is required [S1].
+*   **When not to use:** Scenes with very small Gaussians where proxy approximation is inaccurate; environments without OptiX support [S1].
 
 ## Background
-The method was introduced in the paper "IRIS: Intersection-aware Ray-based Implicit Editable Scenes" (2026) [S1]. It builds upon the 3DGRT OptiX API-based approach [S1] and is implemented within the Nerfstudio ecosystem using PyTorch [S1]. The approach addresses the bottleneck of ray-geometry intersection tests in OptiX pipelines by employing proxy geometry and shader-based aggregation [S1].
+IRIS Ray-Geometry Intersection was introduced in the paper "IRIS: Intersection-aware Ray-based Implicit Editable Scenes" in 2026 [S1]. It builds on the 3DGRT OptiX API-based approach [S1]. The method is designed to address the bottleneck of ray-geometry intersection tests in the OptiX pipeline by offloading intersection logic to dedicated RT cores using proxy geometry [S1].
 
